# Project Details

## Overview

This project investigates the complementary roles of **traditional econometrics and modern machine learning** in forecasting Indian macroeconomic and financial time series.

The central research question is:

> **When forecasting economic and financial variables with small, noisy datasets, does increasing model complexity actually improve out-of-sample performance?**

The study compares classical ARIMA with regularized and nonlinear machine-learning models using strict chronological evaluation.

## Objectives

* Benchmark ARIMA against machine-learning models.
* Investigate the effect of strong regularization in small datasets.
* Develop a methodology for handling the COVID-19 structural break.
* Evaluate historical-price-based equity forecasting.
* Investigate forecast combination using VotingRegressor.
* Build a reproducible time-series forecasting framework.

## Forecasting Pipelines

### Macroeconomic Pipeline

Targets:

* Nifty 50 quarterly average
* GDP Growth Rate
* CPI Inflation

Period:

**2008–2025**

### Equity Pipeline

Stocks:

* HDFC Bank
* TCS
* Trent
* Larsen & Toubro
* Titan

Period:

**January 2022 – May 2026**

The equity pipeline uses monthly adjusted closing prices.

## Core Models

```text
ARIMA
Ridge Regression
Random Forest
Decision Tree
Gradient Boosting
VotingRegressor
```

## Key Focus

The project emphasizes:

* Small-sample forecasting
* Regularization
* Time-series-aware validation
* Zero look-ahead bias
* Chronological train/test splits
* Out-of-sample evaluation
* Economic theory-guided feature engineering
